      "patch": "@@ -19,6 +19,7 @@ All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n - New package [`@threepipe/plugin-annotations`](https://threepipe.org/package/plugin-annotations.html): `AnnotationsPlugin`, 3D annotations like Sketchfab's. Numbered markers on objects with a title, a text and a saved camera view; a click flies the camera to the view. Editable mode to add, edit, move and delete; the edits and the selection are undoable, and `dragOnlySelected` lets only the open annotation be dragged. `closeOnCanvasInteraction` closes the card on any click, drag or wheel zoom on the canvas. `compactCard`: a title-and-text card and a tour bar (‹ title ›) at the bottom that fades while the camera moves; `showCloseButton`. `renderCardContent` lets the app render the card from its values in `Annotation.data`. Saved in the viewer config and in the exported .glb. Example: `examples/annotations-plugin`\n - [`GPUPickingPlugin`](https://threepipe.org/plugin/GPUPickingPlugin.html): `pick(x, y, filter?)` finds the front-most mesh under a point of the canvas by rendering object ids and depth into a 1×1 float target (the camera narrowed to that pixel), with the hit point, distance and world normal (on the side the camera sees; each mesh is drawn with the side of its material, so culled faces are not hit; the interpolated vertex normal, or the flat one for flat shading, octahedral-encoded in the free channels of the same pixel). One small draw, no triangle tests and no tree in memory; for hover and clicks on what is visible. Perspective and orthographic cameras, skinned, morphed, instanced and batched meshes; wireframe materials are hit on their lines, and the global clipping planes of the renderer cut the pick. It implements `IGPUPicker` (`isGPUPicker`, `pick`), the one interface for GPU pickers, so code that picks can find any of them among the plugins. Example: `examples/gpu-picking-plugin` (a marker and a normal arrow at the picked point)\n - [`CanvasRecorderPlugin`](https://threepipe.org/plugin/CanvasRecorderPlugin.html), ported from webgi: records the canvas to MP4 (ffmpeg.js, `FFMPEGRecorder.LIBRARY_PATH`), to the browser's `MediaRecorder` format (`mimeType: 'auto'`) or to an image sequence. Converge mode captures a frame after the `ProgressivePlugin` converges and steps the animations by `1 / videoFrameRate` per frame. A recording keeps the format, frame rate and converge mode it started with (`recordingSettings`). Also `record(action)`, `recordCamera360`, `download360Recording`, the recorders `FFMPEGRecorder`, `CanvasMediaRecorder`, `ImageSequenceRecorder` and `ACanvasRecorder`, and an example. A recording does not start on a disabled viewer or with rendering paused; when rendering stops during one, `record()` resolves with the frames recorded so far and `stopRecording()` does not wait for frames\n+- `CanvasRecorderPlugin.frameSink`: an image sequence gives its frames, in order, to a sink instead of only a folder from the browser's picker: `DirectoryFrameSink` (the folder, the default), `ZipFrameSink` (one zip download, in every browser and with no picker), a function, or an object with `start`, `write`, `finish` and `dispose`. In converge mode the next frame waits for the sink. \"Save frames to\" in the plugin UI picks Folder or Zip download. `registerRecorder(name, factory, match?, ui?)` adds a recorder under its own name, ahead of the built-in ones, with an optional entry in the format list (`unregisterRecorder` removes it)\n - `CameraViewPlugin.recordAllViews()` (and its UI button) records the animation through all the camera views with the `CanvasRecorderPlugin`\n - `ProgressivePlugin.postFrameConvergedRecordingDelta()` returns the recording delta of a `CanvasRecorderPlugin` (it was a stub that returned -1)\n - `ThreeViewer.enabled` dispatches `viewerEnabled` or `viewerDisabled` when it changes (also when the WebGL context is lost and restored), as `renderEnabled` dispatches `renderEnabled`/`renderDisabled`. A wait for `postFrame` can end on it, since a disabled viewer sends no frames"
